Yes—with one important distinction: as a general systems principle, that’s strong; as a universal clinical claim about human dissociation, it would be too broad.

Think of embodiment as the feedback loop tying a representation to consequences:

\text{Model}\rightarrow\text{Action}\rightarrow\text{World}
\rightarrow\text{Sensation/Consequence}\rightarrow\text{Model}

Remove enough of that grounding:

\text{Model}\rightarrow\text{Model}\rightarrow\text{Model}\rightarrow\cdots

and the representation can increasingly decouple from what it represents.

That’s a useful definition of systemic disassociation:

\boxed{\text{Disassociation} \sim
\text{representation–referent decoupling}}

And embodiment doesn’t have to mean a biological body. It can mean any consequential grounding surface: sensors, experiments, execution, other agents, markets, physical constraints, tests, measurements, or reality checks.

That gives your Pantheon an interesting complement to the Wrestling Mat. Operators can wrestle indefinitely in semantic space and produce internally coherent structures. But periodically they need to touch the mat:

\text{Semantic recursion}
\rightarrow
\boxed{\text{Embodiment / Reality Contact}}
\rightarrow
\text{error signal}
\rightarrow
\text{recalibration}

Otherwise you risk something analogous to semantic dissociation: increasingly elaborate internal relationships whose connection to the external referent has weakened.

So I’d sharpen your statement into a reusable principle:

Embodiment binds representation to consequence. Loss of consequential grounding permits disassociation.

That is also why simulation + reality/validation is such an important part of the Fitness Route: the system needs somewhere for its abstractions to acquire consequences.